Implementing Google OAuth2 in a decoupled Spring Boot and React application requires a specific flow. Because your React app needs a JWT to make API calls, we have to use Google to verify the user's identity, and then have Spring Boot generate your standard JWT.
Here is the exact step-by-step process and the code required to bridge Google authentication with your existing JWT system.
Step 1: Spring Boot Dependencies & Configuration
First, add the OAuth2 client dependency to your backend.
pom.xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client</artifactId>
</dependency>
Next, add your Google credentials to your configuration file. You get these from the Google Cloud Console.
application.yml
spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      client:
        registration:
          google:
            client-id: YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
            client-secret: YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
            scope: profile, email
            # Spring Boot handles this route automatically:
            redirect-uri: "{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}"
Step 2: The OAuth2 Success Handler (The Bridge)
When a user successfully logs in with Google, Spring Boot needs to intercept that success, check if the Google email matches an employee in your database, generate a JWT, and redirect back to React.
Create a new class called OAuth2LoginSuccessHandler.
OAuth2LoginSuccessHandler.java
package com.HRMS.HRMS.security;

import com.HRMS.HRMS.entity.Employee;
import com.HRMS.HRMS.exception.ResourceNotFoundException;
import com.HRMS.HRMS.repository.EmployeeRepository;
// Import your JWT utility class here
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.security.core.Authentication;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.core.user.OAuth2User;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.AuthenticationSuccessHandler;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

import java.io.IOException;

@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OAuth2LoginSuccessHandler implements AuthenticationSuccessHandler {

    private final EmployeeRepository employeeRepository;
    // Inject whatever service you currently use to generate JWTs
    private final JwtService jwtService; 

    @Value("${frontend.url}") // e.g., http://localhost:3000
    private String frontendUrl;

    @Override
    public void onAuthenticationSuccess(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, Authentication authentication) throws IOException, ServletException {
        
        // 1. Get the Google User details
        OAuth2User oAuth2User = (OAuth2User) authentication.getPrincipal();
        String email = oAuth2User.getAttribute("email");

        // 2. Check if this email exists in your HRMS database
        Employee employee = employeeRepository.findByEmail(email)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Employee not found with email: " + email));

        // 3. Generate your standard JWT Token using your existing logic
        // (You might need to wrap the employee in your CustomUserPrincipal first depending on your setup)
        String jwtToken = jwtService.generateToken(employee);

        // 4. Redirect to the React frontend with the token in the URL
        String targetUrl = frontendUrl + "/oauth2/redirect?token=" + jwtToken;
        response.sendRedirect(targetUrl);
    }
}
Step 3: Update Spring Security Configuration
Now, tell your SecurityFilterChain to use OAuth2 and attach the success handler you just built.
// ... inside your SecurityFilterChain bean ...

    http
        // Your existing rules...
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
            .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**", "/oauth2/**").permitAll()
            .anyRequest().authenticated()
        )
        // Add OAuth2 Login
        .oauth2Login(oauth2 -> oauth2
            .successHandler(oAuth2LoginSuccessHandler) // Inject your custom handler here
        );

// ...
Step 4: React Frontend - The Login Button
On your React login page, do not use Axios or fetch for the Google button. You must redirect the entire browser window to Spring Boot's automatic OAuth2 endpoint.
LoginPage.jsx
const handleGoogleLogin = () => {
  // Redirect directly to the Spring Boot OAuth2 authorization endpoint
  window.location.href = "http://localhost:8080/oauth2/authorization/google";
};

return (
  <div>
    <button onClick={handleGoogleLogin} className="bg-blue-600 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded">
      Sign in with Google
    </button>
  </div>
);
Step 5: React Frontend - The Token Catcher Component
After the Google login succeeds, Spring Boot redirects the user back to React at http://localhost:3000/oauth2/redirect?token=YOUR_JWT_HERE. You need a component to catch this URL, extract the token, and log the user in.
OAuth2RedirectHandler.jsx
import React, { useEffect } from "react";
import { useNavigate, useLocation } from "react-router-dom";
import { useAuthUserContext } from "../../Contexts/AuthUserContext";
import { Loader } from "../../components/ui/Loader";

const OAuth2RedirectHandler = () => {
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  const location = useLocation();
  const { setAuthUser } = useAuthUserContext();

  useEffect(() => {
    // 1. Extract the token from the URL query parameters
    const queryParams = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
    const token = queryParams.get("token");

    if (token) {
      // 2. Save the token (e.g., to localStorage)
      localStorage.setItem("jwt_token", token);
      
      // 3. Update your Auth Context (you might need to decode the token 
      // or fetch the user profile from the backend here)
      setAuthUser({ isAuthenticated: true, token: token });

      // 4. Redirect to the dashboard
      navigate("/dashboard");
    } else {
      // If something went wrong, send them back to login
      navigate("/login?error=oauth2_failed");
    }
  }, [location, navigate, setAuthUser]);

  return (
    <div className="flex justify-center items-center h-screen">
      <Loader size={48} />
      <p className="ml-4 text-gray-600">Authenticating...</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default OAuth2RedirectHandler;
